{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c62b","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a06ed9-7c63-74b3-b8db-139ac4482e30"},"sequence":368,"recorded_at":1780531400000731,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"680c30af-893a-4c96-ab8b-878d612e53c6","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"680c30af-893a-4c96-ab8b-878d612e53c6","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"680c30af-893a-4c96-ab8b-878d612e53c6"},"call_id":"call_01a06eda264076d3bc19e03e74c5af2b","text":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260830-2890sd/TASK-260830-2890sd_review-verdict-rev5.md`.\n1|# TASK-260830-2890sd — review round 4 verdict (CR rev 5)\n2|\n3|**Verdict: accepted → `accept_cr(TASK-260830-2890sd, revision=5)`.**\n4|\n5|Reviewed the real patch this round. Worktree `.temp/STORY-260830-3jqsx1/worktree`,\n6|branch `task-board/story/STORY-260830-3jqsx1`, base `44a4699`, candidate tree\n7|`3780d471`. I confirmed the working tree hashes **byte-identical** to the\n8|candidate tree before and after every mutant\n9|(`git read-tree HEAD; git add -A; git write-tree` → `3780d471…` both times), so\n10|every measurement below was taken on the exact reviewable revision and the tree\n11|was returned to it. No `git checkout`/`restore` was used; restores came from a\n12|`.temp` copy of the package.\n13|\n14|## 0. Baseline, independently rerun\n15|\n16|| Check | Result |\n17|| --- | --- |\n18|| `go build ./...` | clean |\n19|| `go vet ./...` | clean |\n20|| `GOOS=windows go vet ./internal/provider` | clean |\n21|| `GOOS=windows go build ./...` | clean |\n22|| `go test ./... -race` (repo-wide) | 14/14 packages **ok** |\n23|| `go test ./internal/provider -race -count=2` | ok |\n24|| `go test ./internal/provider -race -shuffle=on` | ok |\n25|| `go test ./internal/provider -cover` | **97.0%** |\n26|| `gofmt -l .` | clean |\n27|\n28|## 1. The three findings handed to me as pre-verified — confirmed, not re-derived\n29|\n30|| Finding | Mutant | Result |\n31|| --- | --- | --- |\n32|| F11 vacuous audit | `Sites[:1]` (1 of N) | **KILLED** |\n33|| F12 / G36 | `Inspect` swallows attester error, uid 0 | **KILLED** |\n34|| F12 / G40 | `CurrentOperatorPolicy` seeds `{0}` | **KILLED** |\n35|\n36|## 2. G-A — the `ownerAttester` seam: **justified, with one measured residual**\n37|\n38|The brief asked three concrete questions. Answers, each measured.\n39|\n40|### Can any production path reassign or observe it?\n41|\n42|No. `ownerAttester` is unexported, in `internal/`, and read at exactly one site\n43|(`OSSystem.Inspect`, `os.go:48`). No production source assigns it\n44|(`grep -rn \"ownerAttester =\"` → the declaration plus two lines in `os_test.go`).\n45|No package outside `internal/provider` can name it. The composed production\n46|artifact is behaviourally identical to the pre-seam one.\n47|\n48|It is also **not a new idiom in this package**: `failDuplicate`, `failInvalid`,\n49|`failPrecondition`, and `failIntegrity` have been production-level `var` function\n50|values since the first revision, reassigned by `TestMain` for exactly the same\n51|reason — to make a production-side fact observable. `ownerAttester` is the fifth\n52|instance of a shape two prior review rounds accepted.\n53|\n54|### Does any test run parallel with one that swaps it?\n55|\n56|No, and this is structurally enforced, not merely current:\n57|\n58|- `grep -rn \"t.Parallel\" internal/provider/` → **zero occurrences in the package.**\n59|- Four tests in `os_test.go` call `t.Setenv`, which **panics** if the test ever\n60|  calls `t.Parallel`. The file cannot be parallelised without a runtime failure.\n61|- The swap is `defer`-restored.\n62|- `go test ./internal/provider -race -count=2` and `-race -shuffle=on` are both\n63|  green, as is the repo-wide `-race` run.\n64|\n65|### Is there a seam-free construction, and is the `var` justified anyway?\n66|\n67|Two alternatives exist and neither dominates:\n68|\n69|- **Inject through `System`/`OSSystem`** (an `attester` field with a nil\n70|  fallback). It removes the package-level mutable, but it moves the same\n71|  weakening target into `Inspect`'s fallback branch: a mutant that swaps the\n72|  fallback is not killed by any test either. It trades one hole for the same\n73|  hole in a different place.\n74|- **Cover the branch on Windows only**, where `fileOwnerUID` always fails and\n75|  the branch is genuinely production-reachable. Seam-free, but unexecutable on\n76|  this host — evidence would regress from executed to compiled.\n77|\n78|The `var` is justified: it exposes a real production branch (fail-closed\n79|attestation, live on Windows) that is unreachable on unix, and it damages\n80|nothing in the shipped artifact.\n81|\n82|### The residual: the seam's own stated invariant is unenforced\n83|\n84|`os.go` says *\"Production code never reassigns it.\"* Nothing checks that. I\n85|measured the cost precisely:\n86|\n87|| Mutant | Result |\n88|| --- | ---: |\n89|| **SEAM1** — production `init()` sets `ownerAttester` to attest every file as the operator | **SURVIVED** |\n90|| **SEAM2** — production `init()` sets `ownerAttester` to attest uid 0 | KILLED (`TestOSSystemEndToEnd`) |\n91|| **CTRL1** — the *same* weakening written into `fileOwnerUID` itself, no seam | KILLED (`TestFileOwnerUIDRefusesWithoutMetadata`) |\n92|\n93|SEAM1 vs CTRL1 is the whole finding: one semantic weakening, killed at the\n94|natural site, survivable through the new seam, because\n95|`TestFileOwnerUIDRefusesWithoutMetadata` calls `fileOwnerUID` directly and\n96|nothing asserts `ownerAttester == fileOwnerUID`. The seam therefore opens\n97|exactly one bypass shape that did not exist before.\n98|\n99|**Why this does not block:** the bypass is not reachable by any caller — it\n100|requires adding a production statement whose only purpose is to disable\n101|attestation, inside the package under review. There is no defeat in the\n102|delivered artifact. The uid-0 shape that LOGBOOK 0230 records as this package's\n103|repeat offender (SEAM2) **is** killed. Recorded as **F18** below with the\n104|one-line closer.\n105|\n106|## 3. G-B — the traversal, with ratios\n107|\n108|**80 killed / 83 measured. 3 survivors: 1 environment-equivalent, 2 substantive.**\n109|Every round-3 killed mutant I re-ran was re-killed.\n110|\n111|### Resurrections: none\n112|\n113|I re-ran **45** of round 3's 59 kills — every substantive gate row across\n114|`Discover`, the name grammar, `trustCandidate`, `OwnerPolicy`, `Trust`,\n115|`Verify`, the `Candidate` absence accessors, `OSSystem`, and the meta-gates.\n116|\n117|**45 of 45 still killed. 0 resurrections.** Nothing was loosened to close a\n118|survivor. Representative re-kills: `index == 0` narrowing on both absolute-path\n119|gates, `allow_path_plugins → if true`, duplicate narrowed to same-source, the\n120|byte-identical duplicate override, builtins hoisted, `pi` dropped,\n121|`sort.Strings` removed, `ExecutablePrefix → \"ax-\"`, every `trustCandidate`\n122|narrowing, `uid == 0 → true`, `OwnerIdentity` constant, all four `Trust` fact\n123|mutants, every `Verify` narrowing, `sum[:1]` / `sum[31:]`, all four absence\n124|accessors, `EvalSymlinks` skipped, `PathDirs` nil.\n125|\n126|### Round-3 survivors: 18 of 20 substantive survivors closed\n127|\n128|| Round-3 finding | Mutant re-run now | Result |\n129|| --- | --- | ---: |\n130|| **F11** refusal-inventory domain floor | B1 empty inventory | **KILLED** |\n131|| | B2 sites truncated to 1 of N | **KILLED** |\n132|| | H4 scan skips `provider.go` | **KILLED** |\n133|| | B5 stray literal + wrong-cwd (empty-but-successful) read | **KILLED** |\n134|| Row 56 `exportedSymbols` floor | derivation returns nothing | **KILLED** |\n135|| (new) `parseProductionSources` floor | returns an empty map | **KILLED** (both callers) |\n136|| (new) `structMembers` floor | derives an empty map | **KILLED** |\n137|| (new) `TestDiscoveryReachesNoProcess` floor | scan finds no production files | **KILLED** |\n138|| **F12** row 40 `ReadDir` failure→absence | `return nil, nil` | **KILLED** |\n139|| F12 row 43 `Inspect` stat error | fabricate `{IsRegular:true, UID:euid}` | **KILLED** |\n140|| F12 row 44 `Inspect` attestation error | `uid = 0` | **KILLED** |\n141|| F12 row 47 `PathDirs` empty entries | empty entries kept | **KILLED** |\n142|| F12 row 48 admin wildcard | seeds `AdministratorUIDs{0}` | **KILLED** |\n143|| F12 row 49 `Geteuid → Getuid` | | **SURVIVED — environment-equivalent** |\n144|| F12 row 50 unix `fileOwnerUID` | returns `0, nil` | **KILLED** |\n145|| F12 row 51 windows `fileOwnerUID` | — | **not executable here (bound, see F20)** |\n146|| **F13** row 14b `Canonicalize` deletion form | `canon = path` | **KILLED** (detail pin) |\n147|| F13 row 15b `Inspect` deletion form | fabricate info | **KILLED** (detail pin) |\n148|| F13 row 29b `Verify` inspect deletion form | fabricate info | **KILLED** (detail pin) |\n149|| **F14** row 11 prefix anchor | `CutPrefix → Cut` | **KILLED** |\n150|| **F15** row 8 partial set | `return out, err` at plugin_dirs collect | **KILLED** |\n151|| | at builtin add | **KILLED** |\n152|| | at PATH abs-path refusal | **KILLED** |\n153|| | at PATH collect | **KILLED** |\n154|| | **at the `plugin_dirs` abs-path refusal** | **SURVIVED — F19** |\n155|| **F16** row 33 empty-owner receipt | owner gate skipped when `record.owner == \"\"` | **KILLED** |\n156|| **F17** row 9 `Builtins()` copy | returns `builtinOrder` | **KILLED** |\n157|\n158|F13's fix is the right shape: the detail pins now attribute the refusal to the\n159|gate that fired, so a deleted gate no longer passes by falling through to the\n160|next seam with the same code. Each of the three kills above names the *wrong*\n161|downstream detail in its failure message, which is the proof.\n162|\n163|### Additional attacks I added this round (not on round 3's list)\n164|\n165|| Mutant | Result |\n166|| --- | ---: |\n167|| production emits a genuine fourth code (`not_supported`) | KILLED |\n168|| unexercised production refusal site added | KILLED |\n169|| stray `Error{}` literal in `provider.go` | KILLED |\n170|| raw `fmt.Errorf` in `provider.go` | KILLED |\n171|| `panic` in `provider.go` | KILLED |\n172|| candidate digest not recorded (`scalar.Digest{}`) | KILLED |\n173|| digest computed over the canonical path, not the bytes | KILLED |\n174|| candidate `path` recorded as `canon` (disguised source path) | KILLED |\n175|| `Verify` owner gate deleted outright | KILLED |\n176|| `Verify` owner gate: `!Approves` clause dropped | KILLED |\n177|| `Verify` owner gate: `identity != record.owner` clause dropped | KILLED |\n178|\n179|### Equivalent survivors: two upgraded from *reasoned* to *proven*\n180|\n181|Round 3 called five survivors equivalent on reasoning. Two are now proven by\n182|construction, not judgement:\n183|\n184|- **Row 36** (`digestBytes` length guard) and **row 38** (`unhex` lowercase-only)\n185|  are equivalent **by construction**: `scalar.Digest` is only ever produced by\n186|  `SHA256Digest` (`hex.EncodeToString`, lowercase) or `ParseDigest`, which\n187|  rejects any non-lowercase-hex digit (`internal/scalar/time_digest.go:132-147`).\n188|  No uppercase or non-hex digest value can exist, and\n189|  `subtle.ConstantTimeCompare` returns 0 on a length mismatch. Row 37 follows\n190|  from the same construction.\n191|- **Rows 42 and 45** remain *reasoned*, not proven — see bounds.\n192|\n193|## 4. Findings\n194|\n195|All three are non-blocking, one-line-scale, and none is a defeat in the\n196|delivered artifact. Recorded for the story's follow-up, not for another rework\n197|round.\n198|\n199|### F18 — the seam's stated invariant is unenforced (non-blocking)\n200|\n201|`os.go:36-41` claims *\"Production code never reassigns it\"*; SEAM1 proves\n202|nothing checks it, while the identical weakening at `fileOwnerUID` (CTRL1) is\n203|killed. Closer, in the idiom this package already owns: either a four-line test\n204|asserting `reflect.ValueOf(ownerAttester).Pointer() ==\n205|reflect.ValueOf(fileOwnerUID).Pointer()`, or an extension of the source-derived\n206|`auditRefusalInventory` scan refusing any production assignment to\n207|`ownerAttester` outside its declaration.\n208|\n209|### F19 — one of five `Discover` refusal returns is still unpinned for partial sets (non-blocking)\n210|\n211|`return nil, …` → `return out, …` at the **`providers.plugin_dirs` absolute-path\n212|refusal** (`provider.go:338`) survives; the other four return sites are killed.\n213|`TestDiscoverRefusesRelativePluginDir` populates its absolute directories as\n214|**empty** (`fake.entries[dir] = []string{}`), so `out` is nil at the refusal\n215|regardless of the relative entry's index.\n216|\n217|I proved the mutant is **not** equivalent rather than asserting it: with the\n218|mutant applied plus one `ax-provider-foo` planted in `/abs/plugins`,\n219|`Discover(\"/abs/plugins\", \"relative/plugins\")` returns **1 candidate alongside\n220|the refusal**. `doc.go` and `README.md:399` both promise the opposite. Closer:\n221|one fixture — give the first absolute directory a real candidate in that test.\n222|\n223|### F20 — the Windows check is never run by anything (non-blocking)\n224|\n225|`os_windows_test.go` is a real improvement and `GOOS=windows go vet\n226|./internal/provider` genuinely type-checks it — I verified that by planting a\n227|type error in the file and watching vet report it at\n228|`os_windows_test.go:32`. But `.github/workflows/ci.yml` runs only\n229|`go test ./...`, `go vet ./...`, and `go build ./...` on `ubuntu-latest`: **no\n230|step cross-compiles for Windows.** `os_windows.go` and `os_windows_test.go` are\n231|invisible to CI, so breaking either leaves CI green. The compile-verification\n232|cited in LOGBOOK is a one-off local run, not a repeatable check — the \"check\n233|present but uncalled\" shape, one level up. Closer: one `GOOS=windows go vet\n234|./...` step in `ci.yml`.\n235|\n236|The README's Windows claim itself (`README.md:407-409`) is supported by\n237|construction — `os_windows.go`'s `fileOwnerUID` returns an error\n238|unconditionally — and LOGBOOK states the not-executed bound honestly. The gap\n239|is the automation, not the claim.\n240|\n241|## 5. Why this is accepted\n242|\n243|- Both round-3 **blocking** findings (F11, F12) are closed, and closed in the\n244|  right shape — F11 with a bidirectional inventory whose reverse check is\n245|  derived from the test run rather than hand-listed, so truncation reddens even\n246|  where the forward check passes vacuously. All four F11 mutants and six of the\n247|  eight F12 rows redden.\n248|- **Zero resurrections** over 45 re-run kills: nothing was loosened to close a\n249|  survivor.\n250|- Every gate that refuses, validates, authorizes, or attests has a **narrowing**\n251|  negative mutant that reddens, not only a deletion form — and the three\n252|  deletion-form survivors round 3 named are now killed by detail pins.\n253|- The delivered artifact contains no bypass reachable by any caller. F18/F19 are\n254|  coverage residuals against deliberate future edits, F20 is CI wiring; all\n255|  three are one-liners and none changes shipped behaviour.\n256|- Repo-wide `-race` green, provider coverage 97.0%, vet/build clean on darwin\n257|  and under `GOOS=windows`.\n258|\n259|## 6. What this method still cannot see (stated bounds, unchanged unless noted)\n260|\n261|1. **Build-tag-invisible behaviour.** `os_windows.go` compiles but does not run\n262|   here. Now compile-verified under `GOOS=windows go vet` — but see F20: no\n263|   automation runs that. Behaviour still needs a Windows runner.\n264|2. **No production caller exists.** `grep -rn '\"…/internal/provider\"'` outside\n265|   the package returns nothing. \"Production entry point\" means the package's\n266|   exported API, which the tests do drive through the real `OSSystem`\n267|   (`TestOSSystemEndToEnd`, `TestOSSystemReadDirReportsFailure`). Whether the\n268|   `ax` host wires `Discover`/`Trust`/`Verify` correctly is a later leaf and is\n269|   **not** evidenced by this task. Unchanged from round 3.\n270|3. **`Geteuid` vs `Getuid` is environment-equivalent here** (`euid=502\n271|   uid=502`, probed). The test pins `OperatorUID` to the effective uid and kills\n272|   the `{0}` wildcard; the euid/uid distinction itself is untested on this host.\n273|   Reported as unknown, not inferred.\n274|4. **TOCTOU.** Discovery reads owner (`Inspect`) and bytes (`ReadFile`) in two\n275|   calls; a file swapped between them yields a digest of new bytes under an old\n276|   attestation. Every userspace implementation has this race and the spec\n277|   records no requirement against it — but this review did not probe it, and no\n278|   test covers it. Unchanged from round 3.\n279|5. **Equivalent survivors.** Rows 36/37/38 are now **proven** equivalent by\n280|   `scalar.Digest`'s construction. Rows 42 (`filepath.Abs` fails only when\n281|   `Getwd` does) and 45 (`Stat` vs `Lstat` on an already-canonical path) remain\n282|   **reasoned, not proven**.\n283|6. **Spec-quote pins read, not mutated.** Quote-anchored with `Contains` +\n284|   section checks and `t.Fatalf` on miss, so not structurally vacuous, but I did\n285|   not mutate `specdoc` itself. Unchanged from round 3.\n286|7. **Mutation coverage is a lower bound.** 80/83 measured over ~54 gate rows\n287|   says nothing about gates I did not think to write a mutant for.\n288|\n289|## 7. Routing\n290|\n291|`accept_cr(TASK-260830-2890sd, revision=5, evidence=TASK-260830-2890sd_review-verdict.md)`.\n292|The element parks at `to-review`; the orchestrator checkpoints or integrates the\n293|accepted revision and makes the `done` transition with\n294|`commit_ack=scope_committed`. F18, F19, and F20 are handed forward as follow-up\n295|for the story, not as rework on this leaf.","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
