{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c911","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a06ed9-7c63-74b3-b8db-139ac4482e30"},"sequence":739,"recorded_at":1780531400001473,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"680c30af-893a-4c96-ab8b-878d612e53c6","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"680c30af-893a-4c96-ab8b-878d612e53c6","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"680c30af-893a-4c96-ab8b-878d612e53c6"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"4e3ee449-0d47-4e15-923c-5406f59e96a7"},"task_id":"4e3ee449-0d47-4e15-923c-5406f59e96a7","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"4e3ee449-0d47-4e15-923c-5406f59e96a7","chunk":"Read text file `README.md`.\n(374 line(s) above)\n375|Section 6.2 is the only configuration section the assigned-scope gate admits:\n376|its single normative clause is enumerated and discharged. Sections 3.2, 6.1,\n377|6.3, 6.4, 6.5, 17.1, 17.2 and 17.4 are refused with their measured ratio, and\n378|Section 6.5 additionally names the `required_capabilities` default defect. See\n379|[Specification-to-Code Ownership Gate](#specification-to-code-ownership-gate).\n380|\n381|## Provider Plugin Discovery and Trust\n382|\n383|[`internal/provider`](internal/provider) implements the Section 7.1 trusted\n384|provider plugin host boundary: deterministic executable discovery, trust\n385|recording, and substitution detection. `Discover` is the production entry\n386|point. It enumerates candidates in the section's source order — configured\n387|`providers.plugin_dirs` in listed order (entry names sorted bytewise within\n388|each directory), then the six built-in adapters (`codex`, `claude`, `gemini`,\n389|`muse`, `antigravity`, `pi`), then `PATH` only when `allow_path_plugins` is\n390|true — and records the canonical absolute path, SHA-256 digest, and approving\n391|owner for every accepted external `ax-provider-<id>` executable. Symlinks are\n392|resolved before comparison, the target must be a regular file owned by the\n393|operator or an administrator-approved identity, and `Trust`/`Verify` carry the\n394|receipt a changed path target or digest must renew.\n395|\n396|If two candidates declare the same provider ID, discovery fails with\n397|`invalid_config` before either is probed or executed; the package has no probe\n398|or execution dependency, and no source imports a process-execution facility, so\n399|the ordering holds structurally. Filesystem read failures abort discovery with\n400|`local_precondition_failed` instead of yielding a partial set, and a receipt\n401|that no longer matches freshly read facts fails verification with\n402|`integrity_failure`. All three codes are pinned against the contract registry\n403|with their exact exits (3, 3, 9). A discovered candidate carries no\n404|availability, status, or capability claim, and the package is an internal M0\n405|host boundary, not a public stable plugin SDK, which the section defers. The\n406|package mutates no durable state: the host persists the `TrustRecord`, and\n407|`Verify` rechecks it. Native Windows owner attestation is unimplemented, so\n408|external executables are undiscoverable there until a Windows owner model\n409|lands; built-in adapters are unaffected.\n410|\n411|Run the focused tests and coverage with:\n412|\n413|```bash\n414|go test ./internal/provider -count=1\n415|go test ./internal/provider -cover -count=1\n416|```\n417|\n418|The package derives its refusal inventory from its own source: every refusal\n419|constructor call site must have an exercised negative path, no `Error` value\n420|may be built outside the four constructors, and the observed code set must\n421|equal the closed three-code set. Section 7.1 citations in its tests resolve\n422|against the digest-pinned document in `internal/specdoc`, quoting the exact\n423|line each claim begins on.\n424|\n425|## Canonical JSON and Immutable Object Identities\n426|\n427|[`internal/canonicaljson`](internal/canonicaljson) exposes the production RFC\n428|8785 JCS entry point and the AX immutable-object identity calculation. The JCS\n429|path validates I-JSON input before canonicalization, including duplicate-name,\n430|UTF-8, surrogate-pair, ECMAScript number-formatting, string-escaping, and\n431|UTF-16 property-ordering rules. Its number behavior is checked against every\n432|finite RFC 8785 Appendix B sample through the production entry point.\n433|\n434|All four Section 1.6 boundary fixtures that publish a digest are pinned:\n(more lines below)"}}}
